The EFE repository ships under two licenses, applied per-directory :

  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────┐
  │ Path                                    │ License                         │
  ├─────────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────┤
  │ /clients/python/**                      │ Apache License 2.0              │
  │ /clients/typescript/**                  │ Apache License 2.0              │
  │ /clients/java/**                        │ Apache License 2.0              │
  │ /clients/go/**                          │ Apache License 2.0              │
  │ Everything else (server, ops, models)   │ EFE Proprietary (see § 1 below) │
  └─────────────────────────────────────────┴─────────────────────────────────┘

Each `/clients/<lang>/.../LICENSE` file contains the full Apache-2.0 text
applicable to that SDK's published artifact (PyPI wheel, npm package,
Maven jar, Go module). Each accompanying `NOTICE` file contains the
attribution required by Apache-2.0 § 4(d).

The carve-out is intentional and reflects EFE's commercial model :
client SDKs are open-source so customers can audit, vendor, and fork
them ; the server, model bundles, operator tooling, helm charts and
proto definitions are proprietary EFE intellectual property.

Boundary rule : if a file lives under `/clients/<lang>/` AND its
package manifest (`pyproject.toml`, `package.json`, `pom.xml`, `go.mod`)
declares `Apache-2.0`, it is Apache-2.0. Everything else is proprietary
per § 1.

Proto definitions under `/protos/efe/` are dual-licensed by reference :
when consumed from a `/clients/<lang>/` build (the SDK proto codegen),
the generated stubs inherit Apache-2.0 ; when consumed from the server
build, they remain proprietary. The `.proto` source files themselves
are proprietary — you cannot fork them and redistribute outside the
SDK-codegen path without an explicit license from EFE.

For dual-license questions : legal@efe.ai

